# Module 3: Windows & Linux — OS Internals

## Windows Architecture

- Widely used for end-point devices (PCs, laptops)
- Designed for user-friendly interaction
- Linux is more prominent in servers and enterprise systems

---

## Modes of Operation

Operating systems use dual-mode operation for security:

### 1. User Mode
- Runs applications
- Limited access to system resources
- Cannot directly access hardware

Examples:
- Applications
- Background services
- Security software
- User-level processes

---

### 2. Kernel Mode (Privileged Mode)
- Full access to hardware and memory
- Executes critical OS functions

Features:
- System-level operations
- Root/administrator access
- Highly sensitive (can crash system if misused)

Components:
- OS Kernel
- Device drivers
- Process management
- Memory management
- File system handling

---

## System Calls

- Interface between User Mode and Kernel Mode
- Allows applications to request OS services

Examples:
- File operations (read/write)
- Process creation
- Device access

---

## SSDT (System Service Descriptor Table)

- Used in Windows OS
- Maps system calls to kernel functions

---

## Windows NT Kernel

- Core of the Windows operating system

Responsibilities:
- Process scheduling
- Memory management
- Hardware abstraction
- Security enforcement

---

## Key Kernel Components

- OS Kernel Core
- Device Drivers
- Core System Management
- Executive Services

---

## Summary

- OS operates in two modes: User Mode and Kernel Mode
- User Mode is restricted and safe
- Kernel Mode has full system access
- System Calls connect User Mode to Kernel Mode
- SSDT maps system calls to kernel functions
- Windows NT Kernel is the heart of the OS
